module gunbc.stage0_seed_retention

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, String, List, Bool }

data stage0_seed_retention_authority_note: String = "THE FROZEN EMITTED-ARTIFACT POPULATION OF THE v1 CUT, and the silent escape hatch it exists to close. DESIGN section 7 requires that each module be either self-emitted green-by-execution or SEED-RETAINED, and that a seed-retained module be a declared row with a reason and a migration trigger, countable and prioritizable, never a silent escape hatch. The v1 cut deleted the v1 .dag authority and the emitter with it, so 136 previously generated stage0 artifacts stopped being generated in one action. Undeclared, they are indistinguishable from the accidental orphan class: live code, absent from the generated-artifact roster that drift-checks it, compiling into the crate. Every individual file looks like the frozen seed working as designed, which is exactly the shape that survives review.

HOW THE POPULATION WAS OBTAINED, because a roster whose membership is inferred is a roster that invented its own subject. It is RECOVERED, not derived: the pre-cut generated-artifact roster at merge-base 8a799b61c4d listed 143 emitted paths; the post-cut roster lists 7; the retention population is the exact set difference, 136. An earlier draft of this work used declared pub mod minus current roster and got 154, which silently included roughly 18 modules that were NEVER generated and are ordinary hand-maintained crate source. Declaring those would have invented an obligation for files that never carried one. The count here is a measurement against a prior tree, not a census of the present one.

WHAT WAS LOST IS THE GENERATOR, NOT THE AUTHORITY, and conflating the two is the error this roster is shaped to prevent. Of the 136, only 62 lost their .dag authority; 74 did not. The split is an identity join, not a prefix guess: every .dag in the tree declares a module name, the emitted basename is that name with dots as underscores, and membership is decided by intersecting the 136 against the 3702 live module declarations. The two arms below are genuinely different conditions and a single frozen label would have hidden the worse one.

THE FORK ARM IS THE SERIOUS ONE. For the 74 AuthorityLive rows the .dag authority is present and editable, the emitted artifact is what cargo compiles, and nothing connects them. EDITING THOSE .dag FILES NO LONGER CHANGES THE BUILT COMPILER. That is DESIGN section 3 dual representation, live, and it is the intended consequence of delete-then-work-forward-on-frozen-Rust rather than a defect introduced here - but a reader who edits dag/std/algebra.dag deserves to learn from this roster that the build cannot see it, instead of discovering it from a green run that proved nothing. An orphan is inert; a fork invites work that cannot land.

MEMBERSHIP ASSERTS RETENTION AND NOTHING ELSE. A row does not say the module is good, needed, or correct - only that its artifact is frozen because its generator was deleted, and that the freeze is declared rather than silent. It is NOT a count: no witness asserts the roster size against a literal, because a number copied from the current tree is a snapshot rather than an oracle.

COMPLETENESS IS ENFORCED IN ONE DIRECTION ONLY, and the other is declared here rather than claimed. An earlier revision of this note said completeness was an identity join in BOTH directions. That was specification-without-execution inside the paragraph asserting this roster cannot rot into a permission slip, and it is corrected rather than quietly reworded because the roster's whole purpose is to stop a declared authority standing in front of an unchecked population.

  ENFORCED - stale rows. w_retained_modules_are_still_declared joins every retained row against the crate's own lib.rs, so a row naming a module the tree no longer carries REFUSES. This is the direction that actually fires: a concurrent cut deletes an artifact this roster declares retained. It is anchored on the exact pub mod declaration and carries a RED control proving it can fail.

  NOT ENFORCED - unclassified artifacts. Nothing detects a previously generated artifact sitting outside every arm. It cannot be checked live: the founding population is the pre-cut generated roster at merge-base 8a799b61c4d, which is a fact about a PRIOR TREE and is unavailable to any witness running against the present one. The consequence, stated plainly: if a later change freezes a 137th artifact without adding its row, no mechanism here notices. That is the same silent-escape-hatch shape this roster closes for the founding 136, still open for anything after them, and it is mitigatable rather than prevented.

  NEXT TRIGGER: an emitted-artifact identity carried on the module's own declaration and checked at ingestion, so freezing an artifact without declaring it becomes unwritable at the point the module is parsed - rather than reconstructed by a consumer that would have to know what the tree looked like before the cut."

data stage0_seed_retention_trigger_note: String = "THE MIGRATION TRIGGER IS NOT RESTATED HERE, DELIBERATELY (operator ruling, 2026-08-16). Every AuthorityDeleted row dissolves on the same condition: v2 self-emits the module green-by-execution. That condition is already owned and already MEASURED by v2.compiler.self_host.frontier, whose rows are built by execution_measured_seed_retained_row precisely so that self-emission is established by execution rather than by assertion. Restating the condition here would create two carriers both answering is-this-module-self-emitted-yet, which is the dual authority DESIGN section 3 forbids arriving through the trigger instead of through the row. So this roster cites that frontier as the trigger authority and stores no self-emission state of its own.

WHY THE TWO CARRIERS ARE NOT THEMSELVES A FORK, checked before authoring rather than assumed. The frontier's population is the v2 COMPILER-STAGE modules - 00_compile, 02_parse, 04_infer and their siblings - and it answers whether v2 has been PROVEN to self-emit them. This roster's population is the stage0 emitted twins of the whole .dag corpus, and it answers which artifacts are frozen because their generator was deleted. Different subject, different evidence basis: one established by execution, one by an operator ruling. That is the External-upstream-decomposition case - one shared shape over independently governed populations - not one concept named twice.

WHAT THIS CITATION DOES NOT DO, stated so its strength is not overread: it is a symbolic reference, not a mechanical join. The frontier carries no per-stage0-basename disposition for the majority of these rows, because most of them are twins of std, gunbc and extdeps modules that its compiler-stage roster never contemplated. So no code here consults it today. NEXT TRIGGER for closing that gap: a frontier disposition keyed by emitted basename, at which point an AuthorityDeleted row can derive its own dissolution instead of naming the authority that would decide it."

data stage0_seed_retention_shrink_log_note: String = "SHRINK LOG. This roster may only shrink, and this row records migrations OUT so the founding population stays legible as history rather than decaying into a false present-tense claim. Founding population 136, measured at merge-base 8a799b61c4d against branch integration/v1-cut, 2026-08-16. No migrations yet: the roster is introduced by this change and nothing has left it."

data stage0_seed_retention_exclusion_note: String = "TWO MEMBERS OF THE 136 ARE NOT RETAINED, and the distinction is a required test on every row rather than a courtesy for these two (operator ruling, 2026-08-16). FROZEN means the authority stops changing while the BEHAVIOUR is still needed and nothing replaces it - that retains. SUPERSEDED means the thing itself is the defect a cut is removing and a named replacement is taking its boundary - that is deletion population and must not enter the roster. If a retention roster accepts superseded modules it becomes a mechanism for preserving exactly what cuts remove, carrying an operator ruling as cover, and every such row would later have to be argued out of a declared authority rather than out of inertia.

BOTH WERE FOUND BY A SWEEP, NOT BY WAITING. Each concurrent cut was diffed against the shared fork base for the stage0 artifacts it DELETES, and the result intersected with this population. Two of the five sibling cuts contest a member. The disagreement is invisible to git: both branches delete the same .dag authority and disagree only about its emitted twin, which one side never touches, so delete-versus-untouched resolves as a clean silent delete and merge order would otherwise decide it.

v1_compiler_ownership - SUPERSEDED, excluded by operator ruling. It is the emitted twin of the per-binding grain, its only purpose is to implement the defect that the ownership-grain cut removes, and per-site verdicts are taking its boundary with consumers rederived rather than reconnected. The frozen carve-out's precondition is that no replacement can hold the boundary, and here one explicitly does, so the carve-out does not reach it.

v1_compiler_compiler_tests_rust - DeletedWithObligationsOpen. It was recorded UNDECIDED and blocking rather than guessed, and asking the lane that removes it produced a THIRD STATE the two-way test does not cover (gentle-dove-833, 2026-08-16). The full reason is on the row; the model consequence is here.

THE THIRD STATE, and why the roster needs it. Frozen and superseded are not exhaustive. A module can go with its region while nothing replaces it AND the behaviour is still owed:

  FROZEN                       authority stops, behaviour still needed, nothing replaces it -> RETAIN
  SUPERSEDED                   the thing IS the defect, a NAMED replacement takes its boundary -> DELETE
  DeletedWithObligationsOpen   goes with its region, nothing replaces it YET, behaviour still owed
                               -> DELETE here, and the debt is counted in the CUTTING lane's carrier

The distinction is not academic, because RETENTION AND OBLIGATION ARE DIFFERENT POPULATIONS WITH DIFFERENT OWNERS. A retention row says someone may still build this artifact. An open obligation says someone must still EARN this behaviour somewhere else. Collapsing the second into the first would park another lane's unbuilt work inside this roster, where its own lane could not see it and this roster could not discharge it. If those obligations are re-established as .dag witnesses this roster never wanted them; if they retire, they were never its rows either. Either way the row belongs to the lane that owes it.

WHY IT IS NOT FROZEN, which is the part a two-way test gets wrong in the dangerous direction: the module is a PRODUCER of Rust test source whose emitted text calls the deleted interpreter, so freezing it would declare an authority in front of output that provably cannot compile. A blocking row caught that; a blanket freeze would have shipped it."

type SeedRetainedModule {
  basename: NonEmptyStr
}

type SeedRetentionExclusion {
  basename: NonEmptyStr
  disposition: NonEmptyStr
  reason: NonEmptyStr
}

